# Git stores text with LF and, where core.autocrlf is on, hands Windows CRLF back on checkout.
# For source that is invisible. For the generated font atlases it is not: their self-test
# compares the file on disk byte-for-byte against what the generator would write today, and the
# generator writes LF on every platform. Left alone, that test asks "are you on Windows?" and
# fails there while passing in CI - which is worse than not having it, because the one thing it
# exists to catch is a stale atlas nobody remembered to regenerate.
#
# Pinning these to LF makes the comparison mean what it says everywhere. The patterns match an
# atlas by its own name and its immediate folder rather than by a path from the root, so an
# application that keeps its font folder somewhere else is still covered - a pattern anchored to
# one application's layout goes quiet when that layout changes, and a silent pin reads exactly
# like a passing test until someone runs the suite on Windows.
**/*.polygons.json eol=lf
**/fonts/msdf/*.json eol=lf

# The atlases' other half, and the fonts they come from. Git detects these as binary on its own;
# saying so explicitly costs nothing and removes any chance of a newline being "helpfully"
# rewritten inside a PNG or a TTF.
*.png binary
*.ttf binary
